"level": "B1",
"el": "Ειδικές χρήσεις",
"title": "Special uses: politeness, attempts, \"would\"",
"body": [
{
"p": "The imperfect has three uses that English translates differently."
},
{
"p": "1. **Politeness**: **ήθελα** softens a request, like English \"I wanted / I was wondering\". 2. **Attempt**: with verbs like πείθω, ανοίγω, the imperfect can mean \"tried to\": **Τον έπειθα, αλλά δεν άκουγε.** \"I was trying to persuade him, but he wouldn't listen.\" 3. **θα + imperfect** is the Greek \"would\": **θα ερχόμουν** \"I would come\", **θα το έκανα** \"I'd do it\". See chapter 33."
},
{
"ex": [
{
"el": "Ήθελα να ρωτήσω κάτι.",
"en": "I wanted to ask something. (polite)"
},
{
"el": "Ήθελα ένα κιλό ντομάτες.",
"en": "I'd like a kilo of tomatoes. (at the market)"
},
{
"el": "Προσπαθούσα να ανοίξω την πόρτα, αλλά δεν άνοιγε.",
"en": "I was trying to open the door, but it wouldn't open."
},
{
"el": "Θα ερχόμουν, αλλά είμαι άρρωστη.",
"en": "I'd come, but I'm ill."
},
{
"el": "Θα σου το έλεγα, αλλά ξέχασα.",
"en": "I would have told you, but I forgot."
}
]
},
{
"p": "**Δεν άνοιγε** = \"it wouldn't open\" (kept refusing). This is the imperfect of resistance, very common with objects that don't work: **Το αυτοκίνητο δεν έπαιρνε μπρος.** \"The car wouldn't start.\""
},
{
"solved": [
{
"q": "Polite request at a shop: \"I'd like a coffee.\"",
"a": "Ήθελα έναν καφέ. | Θα ήθελα έναν καφέ.",
"why": "Imperfect of politeness (θα ήθελα is even softer)."
},
{
"q": "\"The door wouldn't open.\"",
"a": "Η πόρτα δεν άνοιγε.",
"why": "Repeated failure: imperfect."
},
{
"q": "\"I'd buy it, but it's expensive.\"",
"a": "Θα το αγόραζα, αλλά είναι ακριβό.",
"why": "θα + imperfect = would."
}
]
},
